  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Frost or freezing temperatures are possible in \nmany areas tonight and Sunday night.\n\nHigh pressure remains centered to our northeast this afternoon\nand will slowly drift southwestward through early Monday. \nFavorable conditions for radiational cooling will continue the \nnext two nights, albeit with some caveats. \n\nThe growing season for 2026 has ended along and west of the Blue\nRidge and Catoctin Mountains, but continues east of there. Any\nheadlines for frost/freeze conditions will only be issued where\nthe growing season remains active.\n\nOther than some subtle differences, tonight into Sunday morning\nshould reflect a lot of persistence from Saturday morning. This\nresulted in 31-36 degree low temperatures across northern \nVirginia and central and northern Maryland, particularly in \nrural, low-lying areas. Therefore, a Frost Advisory has been \nissued for these areas from 1 to 9 AM Sunday. Felt that \nsubfreezing temperatures would be too localized for a warning. \nAnd while outlying areas along and east of I-95 could be chilly \nas well, the frost potential seemed too isolated for an \nadvisory at this time. Meanwhile, confidence is lower for an \nadvisory in central Virginia due to higher dew points and \npotential cloud cover, as this afternoon's stratocumulus field \ncontinues to drift westward and possibly bank along the Blue \nRidge tonight if it doesn't break apart.\n\nThe high will be drifting southwest toward the central\nAppalachians by Monday morning, with several competing factors\ninfluencing temperatures. Low level flow will turn westerly, so\nareas along and east of I-95 may have a chance of being colder\nthan Sunday without the modifying marine influence. Temperatures\nwill also be rapidly warming aloft, so the ridges and any\nlocation that doesn't completely decouple will likely be warmer.\nThere is also a sizable range of dew point temperatures forecast\nin models. However, the gradient will be negligible with\ndecoupling likely, and the NAM has been particularly consistent\nin showing sharp surface inversions and the coldest temperatures\nof the weekend. All that to say, while coverage is a bit\nuncertain, the frost and freeze threat will persist through\nMonday morning.\n\nOnly minor changes have been made to the previous min \ntemperature forecasts, which are near or below the coldest \nguidance which performs best in radiational cooling (MOS, NBM\n10th percentile and Canadian Regional).\n\nExpect temperatures to rise above normal (upper 60s to lower\n70s) by Monday afternoon as the warming airmass mixes to the\nsurface. Minimal cloud cover and light winds will remain\npersistent.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather continues through next week along\nwith generally seasonable temperatures.\n\nDry weather continues into the midweek period as surface high \npressure lingers to the south Tuesday and Wednesday. A backdoor dry \nfrontal boundary associated with a low over the Northern Plains \nlikely traverses through the region during this time, but no \nprecipitation is anticipated from this as moisture/PWATs remain low. \nAt most, cloud cover begins to increase during this period, with \ndaytime temperatures approaching the mid-70s as high pressure \npersists.  \n\nLooking ahead, a weak disturbance over the Ohio River Valley \npotentially moves through the Mid-Atlantic late in the week. Impacts \nfrom this look minimal at this time, as PWATs continue to range on \nthe dry side while winds remain light. Overall, no precipitation is \ncurrently anticipated for the entire 7-day forecast period at this \ntime. Dry conditions continue to prevail with seasonable \ntemperatures ranging in the 60s-70s during the day, with overnight \nlows mostly in the 40s throughout the week.",
